      "problem": "Evaluate $(1-4)^2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{12}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({{1-4}_{⏟}}_{-3})^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =(-3)^{2}\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(-3)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{9}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & =9\\times \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9\\times 1}{1\\times 12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{12} & = \\\\ \\frac{9÷3}{12÷3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
